 ],
 "literal": "Cool sea's rising wave because of displacing, white-winged / storks in a row having moved, ayirai fish eating. / The town indeed is good, Maranthai, / if in single loneliness one stays, it becomes lonely indeed.",
 "translation": "Because the rising waves of the cool sea displace them, the white-winged storks move in rows to feed on ayirai fish. The town of Maranthai is indeed beautiful; yet, when one stays there all alone, it becomes a place of deep loneliness.",
 "modern": "குளிர்ந்த கடலின் கரையை மோதும் அலைகள் கலைப்பதால், வெண்மையான இறக்கைகளை உடைய நாரைகள் வரிசையாக இடம் பெயர்ந்து சென்று அயிரை மீன்களை உண்ணும். மரந்தை என்னும் கடற்கரை ஊர் மிகவும் நல்ல ஊர்தான். ஆனால், தலைவனைப் பிரிந்து தனியாக அங்கு தங்கியிருக்கும்போது, அது மிகுந்த தனிமைத் துயரத்தைத் தருவதாக மாறுகிறது.",
 "explanation": "In this Neytal poem, the companion (tōḻi) speaks to the hero about the heroine's suffering during his absence. She describes the coastal town of Maranthai, where white-winged storks are forced to move in rows to feed on ayirai fish because the rising waves of the cool sea displace them. While Maranthai is normally a beautiful and pleasant town, the companion points out that staying there alone makes it a place of intense loneliness. The image of storks being unsettled by the waves mirrors the heroine's mind, which is disturbed and displaced by her longing for the hero. Key words like 'taṇ kaṭal' (cool sea), 'nārai' (stork), and 'pulampu' (loneliness) emphasize the theme of separation and waiting characteristic of the coastal landscape.",
